{
  "dom_id": "step-q183598",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Let $x\\in R$ and $y\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\u2200\\,y\\in R,\\,2y=0.$\n- The truth set of the predicate $(\\,\u2200y\\in R,\\,xy=0\\,)$ is $\u2205.$\n- $\u2203\\,x\\in R,\\,\u2200y\\in R,\\,xy=0.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n\u2203\\,x\\in R,\\,\u2200y\\in R,\\,xy=0\n$$\n\nfrom option III reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ There exists a real $x$ such that, for every real $y,$ we have $xy=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since $2y=0$ is not true for some real $y.$ Say, for $y=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false.  The predicate\n\n$$\nP(x):\\,\u2200\\,y\\in R,\\,xy=0\n$$\n\nis true for some $x\\in R.$ For example, \n\n$$\nP(0):\\,\u2200\\,y\\in R,\\,0\\cdot y=0\n$$\n\nis true. Therefore, its truth set is not empty.\n\n- Statement III is true. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,\u2203\\,x\\in R,\\,\u2200y\\in R,\\,xy=0\\,)\n$$\n\ncan be written as\n\n$$\n\u2203\\,x\\in R\\,(\\,{{\u2200y\\in R,\\,xy=0}_{\u23df}}_{P(x)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(x)$ has a nonempty truth set. And according to statement II, this is the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
